<Layout title={title} description={description} canonical={canonical}>
<Fragment slot="head-scripts">
  {schemaJsons.map((schema) => (<script type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify(schema)} />))}
</Fragment>

<ServiceHero
  section="services"
  slug="washer-repair/lg-error-codes"
  eyebrow="LG Washer Error Codes · Diagnostic-First · LA Home Repair · Same Day"
  title="LG Washer Error Codes, Decoded"
  subtitle={`OE, IE, UE, LE, or dE on the display and the cycle stopped. We fix LG WM, WT, and WashTower washers same-day across Los Angeles, Orange, Ventura, Santa Barbara, San Bernardino, Riverside. $89 residential diagnostic. ${phone}`}
  heroImageTitle="LG Washer Error Code Repair"
/>

<section class="section">
  <div class="container narrow">
    <p class="section-number">Answer first</p>
    <h2>Before you book anything: CL isn't an error. And LE, tCL, and CL are three different things.</h2>

    <p><strong><span class="code">CL</span> on an LG washer is Child Lock — a feature, cleared by holding the Child Lock button about 3 seconds. It is not a fault. Don't confuse it with <span class="code">LE</span> (a locked-rotor motor fault, which is real) or <span class="code">tCL</span> (a tub-clean maintenance reminder, also not a fault). For the actual faults, LG's core codes are simple: <span class="code">OE</span> is drain, <span class="code">IE</span> is inlet/fill, <span class="code">UE</span> is unbalance, and <span class="code">dE</span> is the door. $89 residential diagnostic, waived with repair. BHGS #A49573, EPA 608 Universal certified (#1346255700410), BBB Accredited.</strong></p>

    <p>Here's the honest read. Most LG washer faults land in a handful of buckets: a drain fault (OE, drain pump), an inlet fault (IE, inlet valve), an unbalance fault (UE, suspension), a door-lock fault (dE, interlock), and a motor-lock fault (LE, drive system). LG builds a diagnostic mode into its direct-drive machines, and our techs at Same Day Appliance Repair use it on every call to confirm the fault before quoting parts — so you're not paying for a guess.</p>

    <p>Below is the full LG code table (WM front-load, WT top-load, and WashTower share it), then the fixes bucket by bucket. Parent reference: <a href="/services/washer-repair/error-codes/">all washer error codes</a>. Brand pillar: <a href="/brands/lg-washer-repair/">LG washer repair</a>. (Looking for the LG <em>refrigerator</em> codes instead? That's a separate page: <a href="/services/refrigerator-repair/lg-error-codes/">LG refrigerator error codes</a>.)</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="section section-gray">
  <div class="container narrow">
    <p class="section-number">Not-a-fault codes</p>
    <h2 class="section-title">CL, tCL — clear these yourself, no visit needed.</h2>
    <table class="price-table">
      <thead><tr><th>Display</th><th>What it is</th><th>What to do</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>CL</td><td>Child Lock (feature)</td><td>Hold the Child Lock button ~3 seconds</td></tr>
        <tr><td>tCL</td><td>Tub-clean reminder (maintenance)</td><td>Run a Tub Clean cycle; reminder clears</td></tr>
        <tr><td>PF</td><td>Power failure mid-cycle</td><td>Restart the cycle after power returns</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p>None of these three is a hardware fault. <strong>LE</strong>, which looks similar to CL, IS a fault (locked rotor / motor) — see the motor section below.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="section">
  <div class="container narrow">
    <p class="section-number">Full code table</p>
    <h2 class="section-title">LG WM / WT / WashTower washer codes.</h2>
    <table class="price-table">
      <thead><tr><th>Code</th><th>Meaning</th><th>Common cause</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>OE</td><td>Drain fault</td><td>Pump filter (DIY, front-load), hose, drain pump motor</td></tr>
        <tr><td>IE</td><td>Inlet / fill fault</td><td>Supply valve, hose, screen, inlet valve</td></tr>
        <tr><td>UE / uE / Ub</td><td>Unbalanced load</td><td>Redistribute; suspension / sensor if persistent</td></tr>
        <tr><td>LE / LE1</td><td>Locked rotor / motor fault</td><td>Jammed drum, motor, rotor/hall sensor (technician)</td></tr>
        <tr><td>dE / dE1 / dE2</td><td>Door lock fault (front-load)</td><td>Door interlock assembly</td></tr>
        <tr><td>dL / DL</td><td>Lid lock fault (top-load)</td><td>Lid-lock assembly</td></tr>
        <tr><td>PE</td><td>Water-level pressure sensor</td><td>Pressure sensor or hose (technician)</td></tr>
        <tr><td>FE</td><td>Overfill</td><td>Inlet valve stuck open (technician — unplug if seen)</td></tr>
        <tr><td>CE</td><td>Motor over-current</td><td>Motor or control board (technician)</td></tr>
        <tr><td>tE</td><td>Heater / temperature fault</td><td>Heater or thermistor (technician)</td></tr>
        <tr><td>E6 / E3</td><td>Clutch / rotor (top-load)</td><td>Clutch or rotor sensor (technician)</td></tr>
        <tr><td>SUd / Sud</td><td>Excess suds</td><td>Too much or non-HE detergent (customer-side)</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="section section-gray">
  <div class="container narrow">
    <p class="section-number">Drain &amp; inlet</p>
    <h2 class="section-title">OE is out, IE is in — don't mix them up.</h2>
    <p><strong>OE (drain):</strong> the washer couldn't pump out in time. On front-load WM machines start at the drain pump filter — small lower-left front access door, customer-cleanable, clears about half of OE calls. Then the drain hose, then the pump motor ($380 to $540). On top-load WT there's no filter, so it's a hose-and-pump check. See <a href="/services/washer-repair/not-draining/">washer not draining</a>.</p>
    <p><strong>IE (inlet):</strong> water isn't arriving fast enough. Both supply valves fully open, hoses not kinked, mesh inlet screens clear of sediment. If the inlet valve solenoid failed, $260 to $380. The letter is your clue — I for inlet, O for out.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="section">
  <div class="container narrow">
    <p class="section-number">Unbalance &amp; door</p>
    <h2 class="section-title">UE and dE — laundry, or hardware.</h2>
    <p><strong>UE (unbalance):</strong> on one lopsided load it's the washer protecting itself — spread the load and it spins. When UE fires on balanced loads, including small ones, that's worn suspension rods, tired shocks, or a failing balance sensor. Our techs check those before telling you to just rearrange the wash. Suspension or shock replacement runs $440 to $640.</p>
    <p><strong>dE / dE1 / dE2 (door), dL / DL (lid):</strong> the washer can't confirm the door or lid locked, and it won't spin until it does — a safety interlock. First check nothing's pinched in the door boot; if the lock hardware itself failed, the interlock or lid-lock assembly is $260 to $440.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="section section-gray">
  <div class="container narrow">
    <p class="section-number">Motor &amp; technician codes</p>
    <h2 class="section-title">LE, PE, FE, CE, tE — leave these to a tech.</h2>
    <p><strong>LE / LE1 (locked rotor):</strong> the drum isn't turning as commanded — a jammed drum, worn motor components, or the rotor/hall sensor. Technician diagnosis, $340 to $620 depending on the part. This is the one people mistake for CL; LE is real.</p>
    <p><strong>PE (pressure sensor):</strong> the water-level sensor is misreading. <strong>FE (overfill):</strong> water's coming in when it shouldn't — often an inlet valve stuck open. If you see FE, unplug the machine and call, because it can keep filling. <strong>CE (over-current):</strong> the motor's drawing too much current — motor or control board. <strong>tE:</strong> heater or temperature-sensor fault on models with an internal heater. All four need a meter and belong to a technician.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="section">
  <div class="container narrow">
    <p class="section-number">DIY resets</p>
    <h2 class="section-title">Try these before you book a visit.</h2>
    <ol class="num-list">
      <li><strong>Clear Child Lock (CL).</strong> Hold the Child Lock button ~3 seconds. CL isn't a fault.</li>
      <li><strong>Run Tub Clean (tCL).</strong> tCL is a maintenance reminder — run the Tub Clean cycle and it clears.</li>
      <li><strong>Power cycle.</strong> Unplug for 60 seconds (or flip the breaker), restore power, run a rinse-and-spin.</li>
      <li><strong>Drain filter (front-load OE).</strong> Lower-left front access door, pan underneath, clear debris. Fixes a high share of OE codes.</li>
      <li><strong>Inlet hose &amp; screen (IE).</strong> Valves open, hoses not kinked, mesh screens clear.</li>
      <li><strong>Detergent dose (SUd).</strong> Cut the amount, use HE detergent.</li>
    </ol>
    <p>Motor-lock (LE), pressure (PE), overfill (FE), and over-current (CE) codes don't clear from a customer reset when the hardware's down — and FE means unplug and call.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="section section-gray">
  <div class="container narrow">
    <p class="section-number">Honest opinion</p>
    <h2 class="section-title">A year 8+ LG throwing several codes usually has a dying board, not a bad motor.</h2>
    <p>When an older LG shows a drain code one week, an unbalance code the next, and a door code after that, the honest read is the main control board aging and sending false signals — not three separate broken parts. LG's direct-drive motors tend to hold up well, so at year 8 to 12 it's more often the board than the motor. Board replacement runs $580 to $880, which on a mid-tier machine that age is a repair-versus-replace call. We run that math with you at the door instead of quietly selling a board on a washer that's near the end.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="section">
  <div class="container narrow">
    <p class="section-number">Pricing</p>
    <h2 class="section-title">Code-driven part costs.</h2>
    <p class="lead">$89 residential diagnostic, waived with repair. No emergency surcharge — same $89 anytime.</p>
    <table class="price-table">
      <thead><tr><th>Code-driven repair</th><th>Typical cost</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Diagnostic</td><td>$89, waived with repair</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Drain pump (OE)</td><td>$380 to $540</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Inlet valve (IE / FE)</td><td>$260 to $380</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Door / lid lock (dE / dL)</td><td>$260 to $440</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Motor / drive system (LE / CE)</td><td>$340 to $620</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Pressure sensor (PE)</td><td>$260 to $380</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Suspension / shocks (UE persistent)</td><td>$440 to $640</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Heater / thermistor (tE)</td><td>$260 to $460</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Main control board (multi-code cascade)</td><td>$580 to $880</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Warranty</td><td>90 days parts and labor</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="section">
  <div class="container narrow">
    <p class="section-number">FAQ</p>
    <h2 class="section-title">LG washer code questions.</h2>
    <div class="faq-list">
      {faqs.map(f => (<details class="faq-item"><summary>{f.q}</summary><p>{f.a}</p></details>))}
